WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.380
Stress is force per area;

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.340
strain is the resulting change in shape.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.880
A locked fault can resist motion while nearby rock bends and

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:12.200
stores elastic strain energy. Elastic behavior means the material can return

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.600
toward its former shape when the load is removed.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.940
Friction and rock strength help resist slip until the applied stress

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:22.920
exceeds that resistance. The earthquake-cycle graph rises gradually during loading and

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.920
drops abruptly during slip. The graph is an explanatory model, not

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.840
a calendar for predicting the next earthquake.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.780
Rupture starts in a small area at the hypocenter and expands

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.960
along a fault surface. The entire fault plane does not begin

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.760
moving simultaneously. If rupture reaches the ground surface, it can create

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.960
a fault scarp or other surface break.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.520
A rupture may expand farther in one direction than the other.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:50.840
Rupture stops when the remaining stress cannot overcome resistance farther along

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.220
the fault. An earthquake does not have to break every part

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:54.820
of the fault.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:00.100
After slip, some elastic deformation is recovered, but the displacement across

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.880
the fault is permanent. An offset stream or displaced road does

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.040
not return to its old alignment simply because the surrounding rock

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.660
rebounds. Repeated locking and slipping is stick-slip behavior.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.000
The recurrence interval is the average time between comparable repeated events,

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:16.820
not a guaranteed waiting period.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:20.740
Fresh scarps are sharp; erosion rounds their upper edges and deposits

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:24.460
sediment at their bases. Scarp shape and dated deposits can help

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:25.940
reconstruct earlier events.
